`sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSRegression(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that 3 PLS packages\n    # provided in the R language (R-project):\n    #     - \"mixOmics\" with function pls(X, Y, mode = \"regression\")\n    #     - \"plspm \" with function plsreg2(X, Y)\n    #     - \"pls\" with function oscorespls.fit(X, Y)\n\n    def __init__(\n        self, n_components=2, *, scale=True, max_iter=500, tol=1e-06, copy=True\n    ):\n...\n    def fit(self, X, Y):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSCanonical(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that the \"plspm\" package\n    # provided in the R language (R-project), using the function plsca(X, Y).\n    # Results are equal or collinear with the function\n    # ``pls(..., mode = \"canonical\")`` of the \"mixOmics\" package. The\n    # difference relies in the fact that mixOmics implementation does not\n    # exactly implement the Wold algorithm since it does not normalize\n    # y_weights to one.\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        n_components=2,\n        *,\n        scale=True,\n        algorithm=\"nipals\",\n        max_iter=500,\n        tol=1e-06,\n        copy=True,\n    ):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_non_negative`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1336 | def check_non_negative(X, whom):\n1337 |     \"\"\"\n1338 |     Check if there is any negative value in an array.\n1339 | \n1340 |     Parameters\n1341 |     ----------\n1342 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix}\n1343 |         Input data.\n1344 | \n1345 |     whom : str\n1346 |         Who passed X to this function.\n1347 |     \"\"\"\n1348 |     # avoid X.min() on sparse matrix since it also sorts the indices\n1349 |     if sp.issparse(X):\n1350 |         if X.format in [\"lil\", \"dok\"]:\n1351 |             X = X.tocsr()\n1352 |         if X.data.size == 0:\n1353 |             X_min = 0\n1354 |         else:\n1355 |             X_min = X.data.min()\n1356 |     else:\n1357 |         X_min = X.min()\n1358 | \n1359 |     if X_min < 0:\n1360 |         raise ValueError(\"Negative values in data passed to %s\" % whom)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:_generate_get_feature_names_out`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1918 | def _generate_get_feature_names_out(estimator, n_features_out, input_features=None):\n1919 |     \"\"\"Generate feature names out for estimator using the estimator name as the prefix.\n1920 | \n1921 |     The input_feature names are validated but not used. This function is useful\n1922 |     for estimators that generate their own names based on `n_features_out`, i.e. PCA.\n1923 | \n1924 |     Parameters\n1925 |     ----------\n1926 |     estimator : estimator instance\n1927 |         Estimator producing output feature names.\n1928 | \n1929 |     n_feature_out : int\n1930 |         Number of feature names out.\n1931 | \n1932 |     input_features : array-like of str or None, default=None\n1933 |         Only used to validate feature names with `estimator.feature_names_in_`.\n1934 | \n1935 |     Returns\n1936 |     -------\n1937 |     feature_names_in : ndarray of str or `None`\n1938 |         Feature names in.\n1939 |     \"\"\"\n1940 |     _check_feature_names_in(estimator, input_features, generate_names=False)\n1941 |     estimator_name = estimator.__class__.__name__.lower()\n1942 |     return np.asarray(\n1943 |         [f\"{estimator_name}{i}\" for i in range(n_features_out)], dtype=object\n1944 |     )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/neighbors/_lof.py:LocalOutlierFactor.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n258 | def fit(self, X, y=None):\n259 |         \"\"\"Fit the local outlier factor detector from the training dataset.\n260 | \n261 |         Parameters\n262 |         ----------\n263 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features) or \\\n264 |                 (n_samples, n_samples) if metric='precomputed'\n265 |             Training data.\n266 | \n267 |         y : Ignored\n268 |             Not used, present for API consistency by convention.\n269 | \n270 |         Returns\n271 |         -------\n272 |         self : LocalOutlierFactor\n273 |             The fitted local outlier factor detector.\n274 |         \"\"\"\n275 |         self._fit(X)\n276 | \n277 |         if self.contamination != \"auto\":\n278 |             if not (0.0 < self.contamination <= 0.5):\n279 |                 raise ValueError(\n280 |                     \"contamination must be in (0, 0.5], got: %f\" % self.contamination\n281 |                 )\n282 | \n283 |         n_samples = self.n_samples_fit_\n284 |         if self.n_neighbors > n_samples:\n285 |             warnings.warn(\n286 |                 \"n_neighbors (%s) is greater than the \"\n287 |                 \"total number of samples (%s). n_neighbors \"\n288 |                 \"will be set to (n_samples - 1) for estimation.\"\n289 |                 % (self.n_neighbors, n_samples)\n290 |             )\n291 |         self.n_neighbors_ = max(1, min(self.n_neighbors, n_samples - 1))\n292 | \n293 |         self._distances_fit_X_, _neighbors_indices_fit_X_ = self.kneighbors(\n294 |             n_neighbors=self.n_neighbors_\n295 |         )\n296 | \n297 |         self._lrd = self._local_reachability_density(\n298 |             self._distances_fit_X_, _neighbors_indices_fit_X_\n299 |         )\n300 | \n301 |         # Compute lof score over training samples to define offset_:\n302 |         lrd_ratios_array = (\n303 |             self._lrd[_neighbors_indices_fit_X_] / self._lrd[:, np.newaxis]\n304 |         )\n305 | \n306 |         self.negative_outlier_factor_ = -np.mean(lrd_ratios_array, axis=1)\n307 | \n308 |         if self.contamination == \"auto\":\n309 |             # inliers score around -1 (the higher, the less abnormal).\n310 |             self.offset_ = -1.5\n311 |         else:\n312 |             self.offset_ = np.percentile(\n313 |                 self.negative_outlier_factor_, 100.0 * self.contamination\n314 |             )\n315 | \n316 |         return self\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n634 | def fit(self, X, Y):\n635 |         \"\"\"Fit model to data.\n636 | \n637 |         Parameters\n638 |         ----------\n639 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n640 |             Training vectors, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n641 |             `n_features` is the number of predictors.\n642 | \n643 |         Y : array-like of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_targets)\n644 |             Target vectors, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n645 |             `n_targets` is the number of response variables.\n646 | \n647 |         Returns\n648 |         -------\n649 |         self : object\n650 |             Fitted model.\n651 |         \"\"\"\n652 |         super().fit(X, Y)\n653 |         # expose the fitted attributes `x_scores_` and `y_scores_`\n654 |         self.x_scores_ = self._x_scores\n655 |         self.y_scores_ = self._y_scores\n656 |         return self\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/utils/validation.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `sklearn/utils/validation.py` for the full content if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let me explore the structure of the NMF class in detail:\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF`...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF`.\n```\n1120 | class NMF(_ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin, TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n1121 |     \"\"\"Non-Negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n1122 | \n1123 |     Find two non-negative matrices (W, H) whose product approximates the non-\n1124 |     negative matrix X. This factorization can be used for example for\n1125 |     dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n1126 | \n1127 |     The objective function is:\n1128 | \n1129 |         .. math::\n1130 | \n1131 |             0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n1132 | \n1133 |             + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n1134 | \n1135 |             + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n1136 | \n1137 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n1138 | \n1139 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n1140 | \n1141 |     Where:\n1142 | \n1143 |     :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n1144 | \n1145 |     :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n1146 | \n1147 |     The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}` may represent\n1148 |     the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n1149 |     The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n1150 | \n1151 |     The regularization terms are scaled by `n_features` for `W` and by `n_samples` for\n1152 |     `H` to keep their impact balanced with respect to one another and to the data fit\n1153 |     term as independent as possible of the size `n_samples` of the training set.\n1154 | \n1155 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n1156 |     and H.\n1157 | \n1158 |     Note that the transformed data is named W and the components matrix is named H. In\n1159 |     the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite since the data\n1160 |     matrix X is transposed.\n1161 | \n1162 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <NMF>`.\n1163 | \n1164 |     Parameters\n1165 |     ----------\n1166 |     n_components : int, default=None\n1167 |         Number of components, if n_components is not set all features\n1168 |         are kept.\n1169 | \n1170 |     init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n1171 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n1172 |         Default: None.\n1173 |         Valid options:\n1174 | \n1175 |         - `None`: 'nndsvda' if n_components <= min(n_samples, n_features),\n1176 |           otherwise random.\n1177 | \n1178 |         - `'random'`: non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n1179 |           sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n1180 | \n1181 |         - `'nndsvd'`: Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n1182 |           initialization (better for sparseness)\n1183 | \n1184 |         - `'nndsvda'`: NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n1185 |           (better when sparsity is not desired)\n1186 | \n1187 |         - `'nndsvdar'` NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n1188 |           (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n1189 |           for when sparsity is not desired)\n1190 | \n1191 |         - `'custom'`: use custom matrices W and H\n1192 | \n1193 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n1194 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n1195 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n1196 | \n1197 |     solver : {'cd', 'mu'}, default='cd'\n1198 |         Numerical solver to use:\n1199 |         'cd' is a Coordinate Descent solver.\n1200 |         'mu' is a Multiplicative Update solver.\n1201 | \n1202 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1203 |            Coordinate Descent solver.\n1204 | \n1205 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1206 |            Multiplicative Update solver.\n1207 | \n1208 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n1209 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n1210 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n1211 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n1212 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n1213 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n1214 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros. Used only in 'mu' solver.\n1215 | \n1216 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1217 | \n1218 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n1219 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n1220 | \n1221 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n1222 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n1223 | \n1224 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1225 |         Used for initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1226 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1227 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1228 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1229 | \n1230 |     alpha : float, default=0.0\n1231 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to\n1232 |         have no regularization. When using `alpha` instead of `alpha_W` and `alpha_H`,\n1233 |         the regularization terms are not scaled by the `n_features` (resp. `n_samples`)\n1234 |         factors for `W` (resp. `H`).\n1235 | \n1236 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1237 |            *alpha* used in the Coordinate Descent solver.\n1238 | \n1239 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1240 |             The `alpha` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in 1.2.\n1241 |             Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1242 | \n1243 |     alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n1244 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n1245 |         (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n1246 | \n1247 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1248 | \n1249 |     alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n1250 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n1251 |         have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n1252 |         `alpha_W`.\n1253 | \n1254 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1255 | \n1256 |     l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n1257 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n1258 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n1259 |         (aka Frobenius Norm).\n1260 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n1261 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n1262 | \n1263 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1264 |            Regularization parameter *l1_ratio* used in the Coordinate Descent\n1265 |            solver.\n1266 | \n1267 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1268 |         Whether to be verbose.\n1269 | \n1270 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1271 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1272 | \n1273 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1274 |            *shuffle* parameter used in the Coordinate Descent solver.\n1275 | \n1276 |     regularization : {'both', 'components', 'transformation', None}, \\\n1277 |                      default='both'\n1278 |         Select whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1279 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1280 | \n1281 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n1282 | \n1283 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1284 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1285 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1286 | \n1287 |     Attributes\n1288 |     ----------\n1289 |     components_ : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1290 |         Factorization matrix, sometimes called 'dictionary'.\n1291 | \n1292 |     n_components_ : int\n1293 |         The number of components. It is same as the `n_components` parameter\n1294 |         if it was given. Otherwise, it will be same as the number of\n1295 |         features.\n1296 | \n1297 |     reconstruction_err_ : float\n1298 |         Frobenius norm of the matrix difference, or beta-divergence, between\n1299 |         the training data ``X`` and the reconstructed data ``WH`` from\n1300 |         the fitted model.\n1301 | \n1302 |     n_iter_ : int\n1303 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1304 | \n1305 |     n_features_in_ : int\n1306 |         Number of features seen during :term:`fit`.\n1307 | \n1308 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n1309 | \n1310 |     feature_names_in_ : ndarray of shape (`n_features_in_`,)\n1311 |         Names of features seen during :term:`fit`. Defined only when `X`\n1312 |         has feature names that are all strings.\n1313 | \n1314 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1315 | \n1316 |     See Also\n1317 |     --------\n1318 |     DictionaryLearning : Find a dictionary that sparsely encodes data.\n1319 |     MiniBatchSparsePCA : Mini-batch Sparse Principal Components Analysis.\n1320 |     PCA : Principal component analysis.\n1321 |     SparseCoder : Find a sparse representation of data from a fixed,\n1322 |         precomputed dictionary.\n1323 |     SparsePCA : Sparse Principal Components Analysis.\n1324 |     TruncatedSVD : Dimensionality reduction using truncated SVD.\n1325 | \n1326 |     References\n1327 |     ----------\n1328 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1329 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1330 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1331 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1332 | \n1333 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1334 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1335 | \n1336 |     Examples\n1337 |     --------\n1338 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1339 |     >>> X = np.array([[1, 1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1340 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import NMF\n1341 |     >>> model = NMF(n_components=2, init='random', random_state=0)\n1342 |     >>> W = model.fit_transform(X)\n1343 |     >>> H = model.components_\n1344 |     \"\"\"\n1345 | \n1346 |     def __init__(\n1347 |         self,\n1348 |         n_components=None,\n1349 |         *,\n1350 |         init=None,\n1351 |         solver=\"cd\",\n1352 |         beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n1353 |         tol=1e-4,\n1354 |         max_iter=200,\n1355 |         random_state=None,\n1356 |         alpha=\"deprecated\",\n1357 |         alpha_W=0.0,\n1358 |         alpha_H=\"same\",\n1359 |         l1_ratio=0.0,\n1360 |         verbose=0,\n1361 |         shuffle=False,\n1362 |         regularization=\"deprecated\",\n1363 |     ):\n1364 |         self.n_components = n_components\n1365 |         self.init = init\n1366 |         self.solver = solver\n1367 |         self.beta_loss = beta_loss\n1368 |         self.tol = tol\n1369 |         self.max_iter = max_iter\n1370 |         self.random_state = random_state\n1371 |         self.alpha = alpha\n1372 |         self.alpha_W = alpha_W\n1373 |         self.alpha_H = alpha_H\n1374 |         self.l1_ratio = l1_ratio\n1375 |         self.verbose = verbose\n1376 |         self.shuffle = shuffle\n1377 |         self.regularization = regularization\n1378 | \n1379 |     def _more_tags(self):\n1380 |         return {\"requires_positive_X\": True}\n1381 | \n1382 |     def _check_params(self, X):\n1383 |         # n_components\n1384 |         self._n_components = self.n_components\n1385 |         if self._n_components is None:\n1386 |             self._n_components = X.shape[1]\n1387 |         if (\n1388 |             not isinstance(self._n_components, numbers.Integral)\n1389 |             or self._n_components <= 0\n1390 |         ):\n1391 |             raise ValueError(\n1392 |                 \"Number of components must be a positive integer; got \"\n1393 |                 f\"(n_components={self._n_components!r})\"\n1394 |             )\n1395 | \n1396 |         # max_iter\n1397 |         if not isinstance(self.max_iter, numbers.Integral) or self.max_iter < 0:\n1398 |             raise ValueError(\n1399 |                 \"Maximum number of iterations must be a positive \"\n1400 |                 f\"integer; got (max_iter={self.max_iter!r})\"\n1401 |             )\n1402 | \n1403 |         # tol\n1404 |         if not isinstance(self.tol, numbers.Number) or self.tol < 0:\n1405 |             raise ValueError(\n1406 |                 \"Tolerance for stopping criteria must be positive; got \"\n1407 |                 f\"(tol={self.tol!r})\"\n1408 |             )\n1409 | \n1410 |         # beta_loss\n1411 |         self._beta_loss = _beta_loss_to_float(self.beta_loss)\n1412 | \n1413 |         # solver\n1414 |         allowed_solver = (\"cd\", \"mu\")\n1415 |         if self.solver not in allowed_solver:\n1416 |             raise ValueError(\n1417 |                 f\"Invalid solver parameter: got {self.solver!r} instead of one of \"\n1418 |                 f\"{allowed_solver}\"\n1419 |             )\n1420 |         if self.solver != \"mu\" and self.beta_loss not in (2, \"frobenius\"):\n1421 |             # 'mu' is the only solver that handles other beta losses than 'frobenius'\n1422 |             raise ValueError(\n1423 |                 f\"Invalid beta_loss parameter: solver {self.solver!r} does not handle \"\n1424 |                 f\"beta_loss = {self.beta_loss!r}\"\n1425 |             )\n1426 |         if self.solver == \"mu\" and self.init == \"nndsvd\":\n1427 |             warnings.warn(\n1428 |                 \"The multiplicative update ('mu') solver cannot update \"\n1429 |                 \"zeros present in the initialization, and so leads to \"\n1430 |                 \"poorer results when used jointly with init='nndsvd'. \"\n1431 |                 \"You may try init='nndsvda' or init='nndsvdar' instead.\",\n1432 |                 UserWarning,\n1433 |             )\n1434 | \n1435 |         # alpha and regularization are deprecated in favor of alpha_W and alpha_H\n1436 |         # TODO clean up in 1.2\n1437 |         if self.alpha != \"deprecated\":\n1438 |             warnings.warn(\n1439 |                 \"`alpha` was deprecated in version 1.0 and will be removed \"\n1440 |                 \"in 1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead\",\n1441 |                 FutureWarning,\n1442 |             )\n1443 |             alpha = self.alpha\n1444 |         else:\n1445 |             alpha = 0.0\n1446 | \n1447 |         if self.regularization != \"deprecated\":\n1448 |             warnings.warn(\n1449 |                 \"`regularization` was deprecated in version 1.0 and will be \"\n1450 |                 \"removed in 1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead\",\n1451 |                 FutureWarning,\n1452 |             )\n1453 |             allowed_regularization = (\"both\", \"components\", \"transformation\", None)\n1454 |             if self.regularization not in allowed_regularization:\n1455 |                 raise ValueError(\n1456 |                     f\"Invalid regularization parameter: got {self.regularization!r} \"\n1457 |                     f\"instead of one of {allowed_regularization}\"\n1458 |                 )\n1459 |             regularization = self.regularization\n1460 |         else:\n1461 |             regularization = \"both\"\n1462 | \n1463 |         (\n1464 |             self._l1_reg_W,\n1465 |             self._l1_reg_H,\n1466 |             self._l2_reg_W,\n1467 |             self._l2_reg_H,\n1468 |         ) = _compute_regularization(\n1469 |             alpha, self.alpha_W, self.alpha_H, self.l1_ratio, regularization\n1470 |         )\n1471 | \n1472 |         return self\n1473 | \n1474 |     def _check_w_h(self, X, W, H, update_H):\n1475 |         # check W and H, or initialize them\n1476 |         n_samples, n_features = X.shape\n1477 |         if self.init == \"custom\" and update_H:\n1478 |             _check_init(H, (self._n_components, n_features), \"NMF (input H)\")\n1479 |             _check_init(W, (n_samples, self._n_components), \"NMF (input W)\")\n1480 |             if H.dtype != X.dtype or W.dtype != X.dtype:\n1481 |                 raise TypeError(\n1482 |                     \"H and W should have the same dtype as X. Got \"\n1483 |                     \"H.dtype = {} and W.dtype = {}.\".format(H.dtype, W.dtype)\n1484 |                 )\n1485 |         elif not update_H:\n1486 |             _check_init(H, (self._n_components, n_features), \"NMF (input H)\")\n1487 |             if H.dtype != X.dtype:\n1488 |                 raise TypeError(\n1489 |                     \"H should have the same dtype as X. Got H.dtype = {}.\".format(\n1490 |                         H.dtype\n1491 |                     )\n1492 |                 )\n1493 |             # 'mu' solver should not be initialized by zeros\n1494 |             if self.solver == \"mu\":\n1495 |                 avg = np.sqrt(X.mean() / self._n_components)\n1496 |                 W = np.full((n_samples, self._n_components), avg, dtype=X.dtype)\n1497 |             else:\n1498 |                 W = np.zeros((n_samples, self._n_components), dtype=X.dtype)\n1499 |         else:\n1500 |             W, H = _initialize_nmf(\n1501 |                 X, self._n_components, init=self.init, random_state=self.random_state\n1502 |             )\n1503 |         return W, H\n1504 | \n1505 |     def _scale_regularization(self, X):\n1506 |         n_samples, n_features = X.shape\n1507 |         if self.alpha_W != 0 or self.alpha_H != \"same\":\n1508 |             # if alpha_W or alpha_H is not left to its default value we ignore alpha\n1509 |             # and regularization, and we scale the regularization terms.\n1510 |             l1_reg_W = n_features * self._l1_reg_W\n1511 |             l1_reg_H = n_samples * self._l1_reg_H\n1512 |             l2_reg_W = n_features * self._l2_reg_W\n1513 |             l2_reg_H = n_samples * self._l2_reg_H\n1514 |         else:\n1515 |             # Otherwise we keep the old behavior with no scaling\n1516 |             # TODO remove in 1.2\n1517 |             l1_reg_W = self._l1_reg_W\n1518 |             l1_reg_H = self._l1_reg_H\n1519 |             l2_reg_W = self._l2_reg_W\n1520 |             l2_reg_H = self._l2_reg_H\n1521 | \n1522 |         return l1_reg_W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_W, l2_reg_H\n1523 | \n1524 |     def fit_transform(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None):\n1525 |         \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X and returns the transformed data.\n1526 | \n1527 |         This is more efficient than calling fit followed by transform.\n1528 | \n1529 |         Parameters\n1530 |         ----------\n1531 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1532 |             Training vector, where `n_samples` is the number of samples\n1533 |             and `n_features` is the number of features.\n1534 | \n1535 |         y : Ignored\n1536 |             Not used, present for API consistency by convention.\n1537 | \n1538 |         W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1539 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1540 | \n1541 |         H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1542 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1543 | \n1544 |         Returns\n1545 |         -------\n1546 |         W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1547 |             Transformed data.\n1548 |         \"\"\"\n1549 |         X = self._validate_data(\n1550 |             X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32]\n1551 |         )\n1552 | \n1553 |         with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1554 |             W, H, n_iter = self._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H)\n1555 | \n1556 |         self.reconstruction_err_ = _beta_divergence(\n1557 |             X, W, H, self._beta_loss, square_root=True\n1558 |         )\n1559 | \n1560 |         self.n_components_ = H.shape[0]\n1561 |         self.components_ = H\n1562 |         self.n_iter_ = n_iter\n1563 | \n1564 |         return W\n1565 | \n1566 |     def _fit_transform(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None, update_H=True):\n1567 |         \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X and returns the transformed data.\n1568 | \n1569 |         Parameters\n1570 |         ----------\n1571 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1572 |             Data matrix to be decomposed\n1573 | \n1574 |         y : Ignored\n1575 | \n1576 |         W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1577 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1578 | \n1579 |         H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1580 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1581 |             If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n1582 | \n1583 |         update_H : bool, default=True\n1584 |             If True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses,\n1585 |             this corresponds to a call to the 'fit_transform' method.\n1586 |             If False, only W will be estimated, this corresponds to a call\n1587 |             to the 'transform' method.\n1588 | \n1589 |       