    Whether elements in Series are contained in `values`.\n5203 | \n5204 |         Return a boolean Series showing whether each element in the Series\n5205 |         matches an element in the passed sequence of `values` exactly.\n5206 | \n5207 |         Parameters\n5208 |         ----------\n5209 |         values : set or list-like\n5210 |             The sequence of values to test. Passing in a single string will\n5211 |             raise a ``TypeError``. Instead, turn a single string into a\n5212 |             list of one element.\n5213 | \n5214 |         Returns\n5215 |         -------\n5216 |         Series\n5217 |             Series of booleans indicating if each element is in values.\n5218 | \n5219 |         Raises\n5220 |         ------\n5221 |         TypeError\n5222 |           * If `values` is a string\n5223 | \n5224 |         See Also\n5225 |         --------\n5226 |         DataFrame.isin : Equivalent method on DataFrame.\n5227 | \n5228 |         Examples\n5229 |         --------\n5230 |         >>> s = pd.Series(\n5231 |         ...     [\"llama\", \"cow\", \"llama\", \"beetle\", \"llama\", \"hippo\"], name=\"animal\"\n5232 |         ... )\n5233 |         >>> s.isin([\"cow\", \"llama\"])\n5234 |         0     True\n5235 |         1     True\n5236 |         2     True\n5237 |         3    False\n5238 |         4     True\n5239 |         5    False\n5240 |         Name: animal, dtype: bool\n5241 | \n5242 |         To invert the boolean values, use the ``~`` operator:\n5243 | \n5244 |         >>> ~s.isin([\"cow\", \"llama\"])\n5245 |         0    False\n5246 |         1    False\n5247 |         2    False\n5248 |         3     True\n5249 |         4    False\n5250 |         5     True\n5251 |         Name: animal, dtype: bool\n5252 | \n5253 |         Passing a single string as ``s.isin('llama')`` will raise an error. Use\n5254 |         a list of one element instead:\n5255 | \n5256 |         >>> s.isin([\"llama\"])\n5257 |         0     True\n5258 |       