Inverse of DataFrame.squeeze for a\n870 |             single-column DataFrame.\n871 | \n872 |         Examples\n873 |         --------\n874 |         >>> primes = pd.Series([2, 3, 5, 7])\n875 | \n876 |         Slicing might produce a Series with a single value:\n877 | \n878 |         >>> even_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 0]\n879 |         >>> even_primes\n880 |         0    2\n881 |         dtype: int64\n882 | \n883 |         >>> even_primes.squeeze()\n884 |         2\n885 | \n886 |         Squeezing objects with more than one value in every axis does nothing:\n887 | \n888 |         >>> odd_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 1]\n889 |         >>> odd_primes\n890 |         1    3\n891 |         2    5\n892 |         3    7\n893 |         dtype: int64\n894 | \n895 |         >>> odd_primes.squeeze()\n896 |         1    3\n897 |         2    5\n898 |         3    7\n899 |         dtype: int64\n900 | \n901 |         Squeezing is even more effective when used with DataFrames.\n902 | \n903 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]], columns=[\"a\", \"b\"])\n904 |         >>> df\n905 |            a  b\n906 |         0  1  2\n907 |         1  3  4\n908 | \n909 |         Slicing a single column will produce a DataFrame with the columns\n910 |         having only one value:\n911 | \n912 |         >>> df_a = df[[\"a\"]]\n913 |         >>> df_a\n914 |            a\n915 |         0  1\n916 |         1  3\n917 | \n918 |         So the columns can be squeezed down, resulting in a Series:\n919 | \n920 |         >>> df_a.squeeze(\"columns\")\n921 |         0    1\n922 |         1    3\n923 |         Name: a, dtype: int64\n924 | \n925 |         Slicing a single row from a single column will produce a single\n926 |         scalar DataFrame:\n927 | \n928 |         >>> df_0a = df.loc[df.index < 1, [\"a\"]]\n929 |         >>> df_0a\n930 |            a\n931 |         0  1\n932 | \n933 |         Squeezing the rows produces a single scalar Series:\n934 | \n935 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze(\"rows\")\n936 |         a    1\n937 |         Name: 0, dtype: int64\n938 | \n939 |         Squeezing all axes will project directly into a scalar:\n940 | \n941 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze()\n942 |         1\n943 |         \"\"\"\n944 |         axes = range(self._AXIS_LEN) if axis is None else (self._get_axis_number(axis),)\n945 |         result = self.iloc[\n946 |             tuple(\n947 |                 0 if i in axes and len(a) == 1 else slice(None)\n948 |                 for i, a in enumerate(self.axes)\n949 |             )\n950 |         ]\n951 |         if isinstance(result, NDFrame):\n952 |             result = result.__finalize__(self, method=\"squeeze\")\n953 |         return result\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"DataFrame.squeeze\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass DataFrame(NDFrame, OpsMixin):\n\n    _internal_names_set = {\"columns\", \"index\"} | NDFrame._internal_names_set\n    _typ = \"dataframe\"\n    _HANDLED_TYPES = (Series, Index, ExtensionArray, np.ndarray)\n    _accessors: set[str] = {\"sparse\"}\n    _hidden_attrs: frozenset[str] = NDFrame._hidden_attrs | frozenset([])\n    _mgr: BlockManager\n\n    # similar to __array_priority__, positions DataFrame before Series, Index,\n    #  and ExtensionArray.  Should NOT be overridden by subclasses.\n    __pandas_priority__ = 4000\n\n    @property\n    def _constructor(self) -> type[DataFrame]:\n...\n    def _constructor_from_mgr(self, mgr, axes) -> DataFrame:\n...\n    _constructor_sliced: Callable[..., Series] = Series\n\n    def _constructor_sliced_from_mgr(self, mgr, axes) -> Series:\n...\n    # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Constructors\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data=None,\n        index: Axes | None = None,\n        columns: Axes | None = None,\n       